@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+---
+type: adr
+status: stable
+entities: [User]
+related:
+  - app/Http/Middleware/LoginThrottleMiddleware.ts
+  - routes/auth.ts
+  - routes/web.ts
+---
+
+# Throttle login attempts
+
+## Context
+
+`POST /login` was the only credential guess in the app and nothing slowed
+it down: a script could walk a stolen password list through the form as
+fast as the process could answer, and every attempt got the same
+"Invalid credentials." it would give a typo. Credential stuffing needs
+volume, so taking the volume away is the cheapest defence available.
+
+## Decision
+
+The login submission is throttled to **5 attempts per minute per client**,
+by `app/Http/Middleware/LoginThrottleMiddleware.ts` (the framework's
+`createRateLimitMiddleware`), registered as the `throttle.login` alias in
+`routes/web.ts` and applied to the `login.store` route only. Over the
+limit the client gets a `429` with `Retry-After`.
+
+- **Every submission counts**, a correct one included. A successful login
+  is what a stuffing run is looking for, so clearing the count on success
+  would hand the attacker exactly the case worth exploiting.
+- **The client is the connection's own address.** `trustProxy` stays off:
+  nothing terminates TLS in front of this app, so `X-Forwarded-For`,
+  `CF-Connecting-IP` and friends are attacker-controlled and would hand
+  out a fresh bucket per request. Whoever puts this app behind a proxy has
+  to turn that option on deliberately, and only once the proxy strips the
+  headers it sets.
+- **No address means one shared bucket**, not a free pass — the default
+  key generator's fallback. Failing closed costs an unknown-address client
+  attempts it might have deserved; failing open costs the whole control.
+- **Counters live in memory.** This is a single process, and losing the
+  counts on a restart only gives back a window the attacker had to wait
+  for anyway. A restart-per-five-guesses attack needs the server, at which
+  point the throttle is not what is protecting anything.
+- **The throttle runs before the `guest` check**, so a client that is over
+  the limit is turned away whether or not it already holds a session.
+
+Rendering the form is not throttled — only submitting it — so a throttled
+client can still load `/login` and read the site.
+
+## Consequences
+
+A shared exit address (an office NAT, a corporate VPN) shares one bucket,
+so a handful of colleagues typing passwords badly at the same minute can
+lock each other out for the rest of it. That is the accepted cost of an
+identity an attacker cannot forge. Moving to more than one process, or
+behind a proxy, invalidates two assumptions at once: the counters would
+have to move to a shared store (`RedisRateLimitStore`) and `trustProxy`
+would have to be turned on together with the proxy stripping the headers.
